      "writtenOn": "2024-11-27T21:24:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In order to your points:\n* Upstream review: https://lore.kernel.org/all/Z0ZxiLw9hauUynTS@bombadil.infradead.org/ upstream consideres code to be \"mostly ready\", and expects to integrate it by 6.14\n* Great, glad we\u0027re in agreement.\n* (libabigail, libctf, STG) are not present in the dependencies for the upstream kernel today. BTF cannot represent Rust types, which would be nice to do eventually, but doesn\u0027t work today. Doing so would require us to not just implement a system, but convince a data format to select a way to represent things.\n* Compilers, compiler versions, and optimtization levels have had others test them during the development of the patch, but we have not performed systemic survey of all possible configurations. I don\u0027t believe anyone has intentionally tried to downgrade the DWARF version. I would argue that since we hold the compiler revision fixed, and upstream seems convinced of the suitability of consuming the debug information, the most this would come to is the potential of adding additional Kconfig test for minimum compiler revisions or minimum DWARF standards if an issue were detected.\n* Typedefs are type qualifiers are included in hashing. \"Special attributes\" are likely ignored - do you have more details on what you mean there? I believe const arrays work fine, as we\u0027re working on the debug format which essentially represents this as `const { \n array { type } }`, rather than the source code where there is less confusion. Is there something you\u0027d like tested with regards to const arrays? There are two kinds of unused padding - the kind added by the compiler is not considered as a space. The kind inserted or removed for kABI maintenance is managed via macros that send additional directives to the hash generator. While the transfer mechanism (records in a non-loadable section) is different from `genksyms`, it\u0027s conceptually the same as the existing KABI maintenance macros. See patches 13-15 (start here: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20241121204220.2378181-33-samitolvanen@google.com/ )\n* The why is fairly straightforwards, and doesn\u0027t have a doc: `genksyms` doesn\u0027t support Rust, and its strategy really can\u0027t without a large, brittle investment, and as you just stated, we can\u0027t turn off `MODVERSIONS`, so we need a way to make a Rust CRC value. I have a doc where we sketched out the beginning of the how, but that has evolved significantly on the list - the mailing list history of Sami\u0027s series is the most accurate representation of the design process for this series.\n\n-- Break for the non-bulleted response --\n\nThis is what is done with `genksyms` today via various compilation directives. This is not being done as \"automatic equality\", but rather via explicit directives, the same way that `genksyms` works. If you perform an \"ABI compatible\" struct update, you will need to annotate that similarly to how you do today - see the kABI rules section of https://lore.kernel.org/all/20241121204220.2378181-38-samitolvanen@google.com/ .\n\nThis is also fundamentally what *must* be done to be MODVERSIONS compatible - we *can\u0027t* have something where a reversion would be detectable. The CRC loading mechanism just checks equality, not some kind of this-version-or-newer style check. This means that if we have a module that was built against release N of a GKI line and can load on N+1 (the primary goal), then things built against N+1 will load against N. This is fundamental to the MODVERSIONS mechanism, and not related to our CRC strategy. This is also how `genksyms` is working today.\n\nBuilding the code twice like that is not really feasible - if any code in the patch actually *uses* the new field, it will fail to build. The reason a normal `#ifndef` would be feasible in `genksyms` is that they do not actually build the object - they re-parse the source code after macro expansion. Instead, we either use `__kabi_*` reserved field names to indicate things when reasonable, or embed special directives in a non-loadable section in the object file. Your example would be written:\n\n```\nstruct foo {\n  int x;\n  short y;\n  union {\n    char __kabi_ignored_0;\n    int z;\n  };\n};\n```\n\nThis would result in a struct that works identically, and the addition of `__kabi_ignored_0` would be your review-and-audited-change step similar to inserting `#ifndef __GENKSYMS`",
      "parentUuid": "6e341ebb_df85dfdd",
      "revId": "ec0822032e92665882157e9d8b874df12b9e2f78",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
