      "writtenOn": "2024-11-28T15:59:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "**Special attributes.** Things that BTF refers to as type tags. Also, simply, compiler-recognised attributes on types. There is an opportunity to include things like `__user` (which might be `noderef` `|` `address_space(__user)`), if that is not being done already.\n\n---\n\n`gendwarfksyms` directives will handle most safe ABI breaks. One worry is we might be faced with having to add a new case at an inconvenient time.\n\nA recent, non-trivial example is the deduplication of types in aosp/3156610 and aosp/3156819. You will need to tell `gendwarfksyms` that the name of the member should be `unused` instead of `lock`. I think the `__kabi_renamed` functionality will work.\n\nWorse scenarios are things like the a member of type X being deleted along with X being removed from the source.\n\nHowever, I can see that a lot of thought has gone into making it possible to hide ABI breaks and that source code and directives will reflect each instance of this.\n\nOpinion: We don\u0027t want to modify the source in more / complex ways and obfuscate the intended types we get in our ABI extraction when we can just use that abstraction to detect changes and review / audit breaks in a much nicer way.\n\n---\n\nI would like to know why / how other distributions are using `MODVERSIONS`.\n\n---\n\nHowever, I take it back, *we* should kill GKI `MODVERSIONS`. Here\u0027s why (and a little how).\n\n**Temporal compatibility.** The GKI lifecycle has 3 main phases:\n\n* inception to first KMI - there is no tracked ABI, no enforcement, no stability guarantee; vendors will be using locally-built GKIs anyway\n* first KMI to KMI freeze - there is a tracked ABI, enforced, with punctuated breaks in stability; stability means \"only symbol additions allowed\"; we don\u0027t typically play `genksyms` games here, because waiting a week or so to let a breaking change in is acceptable; the KMI generation number (https://source.android.com/docs/core/architecture/kernel/android-common#kmi-generation-number) is incremented at each break point\n* KMI freeze to EOL - the same, except we *do* play `genksyms` games and we are only allowed to break *once more* but have never yet done so\n\nWhat does `MODVERSIONS` achieve for us in each of these stages vs *what we actually want*?\n\n* before first KMI - makes no significant difference\n* after first KMI - we are maintaining the KMI generation number, have a very clear compatibility policy around this but are having to bend our processes, tooling and source maintenance around CRCs, we should just be using the number instead!\n\nDon\u0027t want to load an incompatible module? The module loader should check the KMI generation number (and the `androidA-B.C` release and, of course, symbol presence, and things like the `-4k` suffix which got missed).\n\nWant to monitor ABI stability within a KMI generation? Just use ABI monitoring, *without symbol CRCs*, reviewing potential safe breaks as we have always done (and now with improved mechanisms for review and audit).\n\nThere some complexities around getting the KMI generation (and only the KMI generation number) where we want it, but the simplest thought experiment is: just replace all CRCs with the KMI generation number.\n\n**Spatial compatibility.** Can vendor modules be built for the same KMI generation as the kernel such that they are incompatible with GKI in a way which would be picked up by the current `MODVERSIONS` scheme?\n\nYes, by changing the effective build environment or header files.\n\nHowever, such things are partly under control of the GKI DDK (mandated for Android 16) and we can add an additional DDK build-time check to ensure an ABI match between GKI\u0027s defined symbols and the vendor modules\u0027 collectively-undefined symbols. @umendez@google.com is going to have a look at adding ABI information to the DDK abstraction to support better reporting for missing and type-mismatched symbols.\n\n**Vendor export compatibility.** @vvvvvv@google.com queried the impact on checking vendor module exports to other vendors. This is not something I\u0027ve had time to think about much as yet, but there are a few different directions we could go in, some of which might give partners the freedom to define their own (sub-)versioning schemes for their own exports.\n\n**Rust symbols.** If `gendwarfksyms` can model Rust types well enough to detect incompatible changes, so can STG (and BTF etc.).",
      "parentUuid": "9a256c69_f1328dc5",
